WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.930
There’s a lot of phenotypes that are easy to tell.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.731
Your eye color, your hair texture, your height,

00:00:13.781 --> 00:00:16.950
whether you have a straight thumb or hitchhiker thumb.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.721
But one phenotype that you can’t just tell by looking is your blood type.

00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:26.950
Your blood is really made of many things—platelets, plasma, and red blood cells.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.640
But you have probably heard before that when blood is donated,

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:31.950
it’s important that it

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.354
is matched correctly? And that’s true, because blood type phenotypes vary.

00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:42.321
It really boils down to the fact that red blood cells are not naked.

00:00:42.371 --> 00:00:44.950
They have proteins on their surface.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.101
And it turns out that your immune system is very protective and if

00:00:49.151 --> 00:00:53.692
it gets blood donated that have different proteins that it’s not used to,

00:00:53.742 --> 00:00:54.950
it will attack them!

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:01.768
With blood type, you can have several different phenotypes: A, B, AB, or O.

00:01:01.818 --> 00:01:02.950
These letters

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.564
stand for antigens that are found on red blood cells.

00:01:06.614 --> 00:01:08.950
So type A blood, for example, has A

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.510
antigens on the surface of red blood cells.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.950
Type B blood, for example, has B antigens

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.268
on the surface of red blood cells.

00:01:16.318 --> 00:01:19.950
Type AB blood has both A and B antigens on the surface

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.194
of red blood cells.

00:01:21.244 --> 00:01:26.694
Type O—I like to think of it is looking like a zero—it doesn’t have A or B antigens.

00:01:26.744 --> 00:01:30.950
It’s naked! Well, ok, it does have other proteins on its surface.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.950
But not A or B. So think of O as looking like a zero! It doesn't have A or B.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.950
So if you are type B blood, you have B antigens on the surface of red blood cells. That means,

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.913
a person with type B can accept another person's type

00:01:46.963 --> 00:01:50.950
B blood because B is an antigen their body recognizes.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.950
But if you try to give that person a type A blood type, that's an antigen that

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.657
the immune system does not recognize. That person's immune system will attack!

00:02:00.707 --> 00:02:02.950
It would also attack AB blood, because

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.886
that includes the A that it doesn’t recognize. Now Type O would be safe though.

00:02:08.936 --> 00:02:13.470
Remember O looks like a zero—it doesn’t have A or B antigens.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.950
So type O can donate to everyone!

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.015
Now while O individuals can donate to everyone,

00:02:19.065 --> 00:02:21.950
they can only receive blood from another type

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:22.105
O.

00:02:22.155 --> 00:02:26.062
Because type O blood does not have A or B antigens,

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:30.950
their immune system will attack any other blood type that does.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.634
Neither of us have type AB blood,

00:02:32.684 --> 00:02:35.950
but we would think this is a cool blood type to have in the sense

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.764
that you could receive blood from anyone.

00:02:38.814 --> 00:02:42.950
If an AB person received blood from a person that had Type A,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.950
well they’ve got the "A" so it’s all good. And if an AB person received blood from

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.516
a person that had type B, well they’ve got the B antigen too, so it’s all good.

00:02:53.566 --> 00:02:58.950
They can receive blood from type O too because there are not any antigens to even worry about.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.950
Now one thing we want to add that makes all of this a bit more complicated—blood types

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:10.950
also have a plus or minus sign listed by the blood type. This makes a big difference

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.130
with blood donations.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.107
If you have a plus, it means that you have this other

00:03:15.157 --> 00:03:18.871
little protein called Rh factor on the surface of your blood cells.

00:03:18.921 --> 00:03:20.950
If you have a negative, it means that

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.550
you do not have this little protein called Rh factor on the surface of your red blood cells.
